A||||6||pHoOl||Swapping a factory Sub||||3||
Z||000000||pHoOl||07-24-2000||12:29 AM||||I found a guy in my area who bought an aftermarket stereo and hasn't figured out a way to hook up his factory sub.  If i were to buy it off of him, what is involved in the install?  I know that there is some sort of switch above the map lights, but didn't get a good look to see if it's just a standard on/off, or a pot.  Also, is there an amp buried somewhere in the car that i'd have to take also?  And finally, is it worth a couple of bucks to get the factory sub, I mean, is it noticeable, or just nice to say that you have a sub in your fiero?||||reg||3
Z||000001||wkayl||07-24-2000||12:48 AM||||I have one in my 86 GT.  I think it sounds good (but then again I'm not in my 20's anymore).  Here is a link that may help you.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/sub.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/sub.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||07-24-2000||03:44 AM||||Id buy if if I were you....<BR>The switch overhead is a "sub volume" of sorts....has "MIN" on left and "MAX" on right and you can slide the switch between the two.<BR>Yes there is an amp hidden in there (somewhere, dont ask me where)<BR>If his speaker isnt blown by now, it'll be the first one Ive ever seen that isnt! But, they can be repaired.........<BR>They give a pretty good sound (from what Ive heard people say, like I said, all mine are blown)...but I wouldnt think it'd handle all that silly over based music thats out today. That kinda music really wasnt around, or not as popular, back in the 80s (ahhh, the good ole' days!)....but Im sure it helps out.<BR>The speaker itself is on the pass. side, just under the dash....really not too noticable..... <p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||07-24-2000||06:32 AM||||The amp is located under a plastic shroud under the carpeting on the passenger side of the console.  Personally I am on the other side of the fence as 7 Fieros on this one. I think the factory sub is just hype. Also the amount of diisassembly of the interior for removal and replacement is excessive an will likely damage one of the two cars f you are inexperienced in stripping Fieros. I say save your time and money, get good speakers and a good amp.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||07-24-2000||09:24 AM||||Interesting that you should bring this topic up. I just installed a complete factory unit into my car. I may not have the "BOOM, BOOM, BOOM" that the young people want today, but I like to have as many factory options as I can get my hands on.<P>To remove and install, you have to EXTENSIVELY disassemble the interior. The wires are all plug-n-play but they run all over the interior.<P>To remove the wires feeding the overhead control panel: Remove the control panel; remove the headliner (good time to have it recovered); remove the driver's side a-piller molding.<P>To remove the main wireing harness: remove the dash (you will have to remove the center console, and dash pod to do this); unplug the harness from the plug behind and above the fuse panel; remove the wiring from the harness clips; unplug the connector behind the radio.<P>To remove the booster box: remove the screw on the left and the push pin on the right of the box.<P>To remove the amplifier: Pull the carpet back on the passenger side just below the center control pod; Follow the wiring behind the plastic side curtian; remove the screw securing the amplifer; unsnap the side curtain to remove the amplifier.<P>It's a lot of work, but can be completed in a day. I took the whole weekend and cleaned my carpet and seats while they were out (not required but I removed the seats to have more working room). I also took aditional time to replace a couple of items that were not working properly or were broken. It's also a goodtime to replace the front speakers with 6x9s if that's something you were planning for.<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||Seanh||07-24-2000||02:06 PM||||Well. I have an 86 GT. And Im a stero Installer. First of all, for no more then your going to pay for the amp and 5.25in sub and enclosure, I think Its worth the money and time to do it. BUT, have you checked to make sure that amp will fit in the same place it is on his car? It might not, but you could always mount it some place else, like behind the seats. Thats where I mounted all my amp and master units for my Pioneer system. If your going to do this, email me back and Ill send you a wiring diagram for this system. It might be kinda confusing, but once you understand it, It will help alot. Theres really not that much to this strange system. Oh yes, and the slider on the map lights is only a variable 10K resistor hooked up in sieries with a fixed 10K resistor, both can be purchased at Radio Shack, just tell them what you need. If you do this, It will be eaisier to mount beside the cig. lighter in the middle.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||07-24-2000||03:32 PM||||Somebody mentioned putting 6x9's in the front.  How much modification has to be made for these to fit?  last time i looked in there, it didn't seem like there would be room to do anything but the 4x10s.  BTW, how much bass do the 6x9's get?  I'm 19, so i'd like a little bit more than what the factory ones have, but i dont' need to vibrate my engine off the mounts like all of the other kids my age seem to think is necessary.  I like having a car that isn't heard five miles away.||||reg||1
